  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/...\nIssued 254 PM MDT THU OCT 22 2026\nA short lived high pressure system will continue to build in\nover the region Sunday, bringing slightly warmer temperatures \nand dry conditions through Monday morning. Clear skies overnight\npaired with the recent precipitation will allow for valley fog \nto form late Sunday night and again on Monday night, but it \nshould dissipate by the late morning. A cut off low pressure \nsystem from the Gulf of Alaska will move in late Monday-early \nTuesday, bringing widespread precipitation and cooler \ntemperatures to the region. Disagreement in the timing of the \nlow pressure system exists, with most models pushing \nprecipitation and cooler temperatures late Monday, while a few \noutlier solutions delay the arrival to Tuesday morning. Current \nforecast favors high terrain in northeast Oregon and central \nIdaho for the best chance of precipitation (30-50% chance) \nthrough Thursday, with snow levels anticipated to be around \n6000-7000 feet. The low will then push to the east, allowing for\nanother ridge of high pressure to build in by Thursday, \nbringing another slight warming and drying trend for the region.",
